  "notes": "Toronto's Motown-revivalist soul voice. `confidence` is **low** — the research rates the entry medium-low, sourcing the retro-soul lane position but marking \"tempo, drum and harmonic detail\" as inference. The drums are the stated 60s kit: \"dry, tight 60s-style kit with the snare cracked on 2&4, tambourine doubling it, no 16th hat carpet, and triplet fills into sections\". That last clause is the one field that separates her from the other retro-soul models here — `snareRoll.subdivisions` are triplets (16t and 8t) rather than straight 16ths — and the tambourine mirrors the clap over a downbeat-placed percussion set. Harmony is \"major-mode 60s-soul diatonicism — `I–vi–IV–V` and 12-bar-derived forms with dominant 7ths\", so both families are the ones the entry writes out and the triad weight is high with almost no ninths. Melody is \"mid-alto with a hard-edged belt, short repeated phrases, gospel ornament, and group-vocal answering lines on the chorus\", which is the call-and-response structure with two-and-four-bar phrases and a dense answering counter. Bass is \"Jamerson-lineage melodic 8ths on the soul material, and long root-and-fifth lovers-rock lines on the reggae-facing cuts\". ⚠ Every drum, melody and bass number here is INFERRED. ⚠ The reggae variant the entry names — kick and snare together on beat 3 — is **not** encoded: this model states the straight 2&4 spine only, and a one-drop would need a placement change the Lane line does not carry.",
